united in their utter  hatred for the movie  for example dason thompson of the  washington post called it  breathtakingly unadventurous with its  cheesy storyline  hackneyed ballpark fable sentiments  adorable animal  and collection of one-dimensional human  nincompoops  in the early 1980s chuck norris was the  king of a certain kind of patriotic war  movie  in which the star could bomb shoot and  karate kick bad guys to his heart's  content  the success of missing in action in the  delta force would eventually lead to  norris playing a mystical kick happy  lawman  on the 90s action show walker texas  ranger this thing led to a revival of  his film career which by then had taken  a bit of a downward turn  this was largely because norris's style  of movie had grown a little passe  so filmmakers tried to open things up by  sticking him in a family comedy  the result is top dog in which norris  plays a loose cannon cop who gets teamed  up with a super smart police canine  top dog is supposed to appeal to kids  but it's also casually and extremely  violent  following a plot about extremist terror  attacks critics loathed the movie too  which wound up with a zero percent on  rotten tomatoes as roger ebert asked in  his chicago sun times review  why exactly would you want to make a  movie about racist hate groups and then  disguise it as family entertainment  about a cute dog  launch to stardom in the late 1980s with  the movie i'm gonna get you sucker and  then in the 90s with the sketch comedy  series in living color  filmmaker and actor keenan ivory wayne  struggled to find a follow-up project  that was a good match for his talents  his two stabs at action heroism most  wanted and the glimmerman  both bombed but at least critics had a  few nice things to say about those  movies  the same can't be said for wayne's 1994  romantic action comedy a low down dirty  shame  wayne's wrote and directed the oddly  laid back movie about andre shane  a disgraced police detective in private  eye who just can't seem to bust drug  kingpin mendoza  suddenly shane finds a new lead and gets  back on the case with the help of his  sassy assistant peaches  austin chronicle critic alison macor  summed up the movie best writing  i'm not sure what type of movie this is  supposed to be or who its audience is  but it's a low down dirty shame that  wayan's talent is wasted on this film  some of the movies that have tried and  failed to reach the critical and box  office highs of the original jaws  include deep blue sea the mini sharknado  movies  and jaws own sequels you're gonna need a  bigger boat  making a movie like this is like walking  a tightrope over a proverbial shark tank  if it's too violent salacious or gory  then it's an outright bad movie  if it goes too far in the other  direction instead focusing on tension  and dread then it's usually perceived as  little more than a jaw's rip-off  somehow dark tide manages to be both  halle berry plays a shark expert and  diving teacher who has to guide a  daredevil rich guy  into a part of the ocean so infested  with sharks that it's nicknamed  shark alley you can probably guess what  happens next  as peter bradshaw wrote in the guardian  the sharks themselves are the only ones  to emerge with credit from this  hollywood foisted seven police academy  movies onto the world in the 80s and 90s  despite  only the first being a true blockbuster  and all of them getting  absolutely savage by the critics as far  as rotten tomato scores are concerned  police academy is among the most  critically loathed and poorly reviewed  franchises in movie history  entries four through seven citizens on  patrol assignment miami beach  city under siege and mission to moscow  are all rated at zero percent on the  site  operating under the premise that a big  city police department has to sign up  virtually anyone who wishes to protect  and serve  these particular installments find the  cadets training new hires  traveling to miami fighting an organized  crime ring and trying to take down a  money launderer in moscow  ian freer of empire in his review of  police academy 4  citizens on patrol summed up the  thoughts of his fellow critics with  regards to the whole series  he wrote oh dear oh dear oh dear  tom selleck got his movie career going  in 1987 with the smash hit  three men and a baby sadly he didn't  have many more hits beyond that  although he did offer up a kind of  reversal on three men and a baby with  the 1992 comedy  folks in which he played a yuppie trying  to contend with his aging parents  supposedly a comedy folks is basically a  nasty 100 minute insult against  well-meaning elderly people  select plays jon who invites his  increasingly infirm parents to move in  with him and his family after his father  burns down their home however  his folks meddling somehow causes  celic's character to lose his job  as well as his wife and kids in his  review for the seattle times  michael upchurch wrote the film's appeal  will depend largely on whether you feel  like laughing at see now dementia  and that about sums it up the d-grade  e.t rip-off mac and me  is noteworthy for just a few reasons  firstly it contains perhaps the most  egregious use of product placement in  hollywood history with an entire scene  being devoted to an amazing birthday  party held at mcdonald's  that even features ronald mcdonald  himself it's also a dance number  which e.t is notably lacking so at least  mcamee brought something new to the  table  but that's about it honestly the only  real reason anyone might have to watch  this silly 80s flick is to get some  context for a running gag  paul rudd has been doing for years every  time he guests on conan o'brien's show  to promote a film  he switches out the clip of that film  with the same clip from mac and me  [Music]  he's been doing it for over 10 years now  and to his credit it's actually a pretty  good bit which is more than can be said  for the movie itself  stories about secret government  conspiracies were a big deal in the 90s  unfortunately for every all-time great  like x-files or enemy of the state  you had well you had something like  shadow conspiracy  charlie sheen at the time a legitimate  movie star after the success of platoon  and hot shots  stars as bobby bishop an innocent and  earnest presidential aide  who discovers a plot orchestrated by the  white house chief of staff  to assassinate the commander-in-chief  people get killed left and right as a  result  and it's up to bobby and a plucky  reporter to put an end to the threat  from within  the problem is shadow conspiracy isn't  quite as clever as it thinks it is  according to maitland mcdonough of tv  guide corpses pile up  dreadful secrets are uncovered and  bishop eventually gets to the bottom of  things  a full hour after the least attentive  viewer will have figured out who's  behind it all  in elementary school millions of  american kids learn about the country's  homegrown folklore  19th century tall tales about  larger-than-life figures like the  lumberjack giant paul bunyan  and john henry in the tales henry is a  hulk-like ultra-strong railroad worker  known as the steel driving man the 2020  movie john henry however reimagines the  character as a tough modern day man who  uses his abilities to reluctantly right  wrongs  terry crews plays the titular john henry  who is trying to get over his violent  past when he runs afoul of his old crime  boss after helping two honduran  immigrants escape gang violence  movie critics weren't exactly moved by  john henry however collectively  bestowing it with a zero percent on  rotten tomatoes  jeanette katsulus of the new york times  said no kindness goes unpunished in john  henry  a thug life thriller so frequently  preposterous that it almost resembles a  parody  while michael retrofon of the los  angeles times said john henry lands with  all the subtlety of the mighty still  driving man sledgehammer  flash fancy cars driven by a rogue's  gallery of cocky tight-knit characters  in a world of underground street racing  those are the elements that help make  the fast and the furious franchise  so irresistible and lucrative to  audiences around the world  obviously the filmmakers behind redline  hoped the market was big enough for  another movie of that ilk  only with less money and fewer famous  cast members  unfortunately redline grossed the  poultry 8.2 million dollars  largely because the vast majority of the  movie going public  immediately recognized it for what it  was a shameless  unabashed not very good rip-off of the  fast and the furious saga  not convinced check out this atrocious  scene  [Music]  okay we pulled a rut but critics loathe  the real movie  which currently sits at an impressive  zero percent on rotten tomatoes  as j.r jones of the chicago reader so  succinctly explains  the whole thing is pretty stupid there  are a lot of haunted house movies out  there  to say the least it's a tried and true  film sub-genre  and they really do feel a niche mostly  because audiences love the familiar  beats of stories about things that go  bump in the night  now movie critics traditionally aren't  all that keen on horror movies  but it's still pretty hard to make one  so bad that it's panned by  literally everybody but that's exactly  what happened with the disappointments  room  in this movie a woman named dana and her  family move out of busy brooklyn  in favor of a house in the country it's  already occupied however  by secrets ghosts and plenty of  foreboding dread  all thanks to the family that lived  there in the 1800s  robert beale of the rap said the movie  consists of a litany of embarrassments  while mark savlov of the austin  chronicle wrote that the disappointments  room  lives and dies up to its name  conventional wisdom holds that there are  two kinds of successful movies  box office smashes and critical darlings  there actually isn't a lot of disparity  between the two  blockbusters are often praised by  critics while award winners can get a  serious financial boost from their  plotters  and the movies that critics hate often  bomb at the box office too  after all a dud is a dud but then  there's problem child  a chaotic cynical snotty and somehow  also sentimental comedy with a zero  percent rotten tomatoes  that made an absolute fortune at the box  office  john ritter a beloved tv star who  couldn't really get a legitimate movie  career going plays sweet ben  a man desperate to be a father but  unable to have one biologically  so he adopts junior an almost  demonically bratty kid  who makes life horrible for ben as  dennis king of the tulsa world wrote  it's misguided it's not funny and in its  lame efforts to be irreverent in hip  it turns out to be just plain mean  as noted earlier bruce willis made a  name for himself playing tough guys and  reluctant heroes in action movies during  the 1980s and 1990s a golden age for the  genre  it's been a long time since the release  of die harder armageddon however  and film goers have mostly moved on to  other things but for a small but devoted  audience who still wants to see the  60-something action icon do his thing  he still makes a few low budget action  flicks each year all of which get  quietly dumped onto home video and  streaming platforms  hard kill is an especially bad example  of these late period willis movies  the story of hired guns trying to stop a  supervillain from using computers to  cause worldwide chaos  filming on this movie took place over  just 10 days if nothing else however  that means hardkill managed to earn its  zero percent rotten tomato score with  the utmost efficiency  london fields actually starts out with  an intriguing premise  a psychic named nicola 6 predicts that  she is about to be killed  her sixth sense tells her that her  killer will be one of three men so she  starts an affair with each in order to  discover the true circumstances of her  death  london fields is based on a darkly comic  critically acclaimed 1989 novel by  british mystery author  martin amos but the adaptation  apparently failed to capture what was so  special about the book  peter sobsinski of robert ebert.com said  that even viewers who know the novel  well will have a hard time following the  convoluted action of what amounts to a  boring and garish mess  david sexton of the london evening  standard meanwhile likened london fields  to a music video  while jeanette kat solis of the new york  times found it horrendous  a trashy torch and misfire from  beginning to end still  it could have been worse  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  the best and worst movies are coming  soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

